{"toolCallId":"call_55ea725b842641fe866d8a7528336332","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Write three sentences: the problem, the change and the proof. GOOD: Callers parse a 17-line report to read one value. The flag prints one line. A line count proves it. WEAK: This document describes the feature. -->\n\n[2-3 sentence high-level overview for stakeholders who need quick context]\n\n**Key Decisions**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 1], [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 2]\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Blocking dependency]\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/recommend-level-quiet`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Callers that need only the recommended level must scrape a 16-line report, and --json answers with a 36-line document instead of a shorter one. A --quiet flag prints the existing level line and nothing else, while every other invocation stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"recommend-level quiet mode\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"implementation\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` answers one question — which documentation level a task needs — but it prints a 16-line report to answer it. A build script that wants the answer must scrape the second line, and `--json` replaces the report with a 36-line document rather than shortening it. This packet adds a `--quiet`/`-q` flag that prints only `Recommended Level: N (Name)`, leaves every other invocation byte-identical, and is proven by line counts on both paths.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` is a flag rather than an environment variable; it reuses the existing level line verbatim; `--json` keeps precedence over `--quiet`\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: Bash 3.2 or newer (the macOS default), which the script already requires; the change adds no new dependency\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nThe script prints 16 lines for `--loc 500 --files 15` and 36 lines for `--json`, both measured against the current file. A caller that needs the level must pipe the report through `head`, `grep` or a JSON parser. Today `--quiet` is not an option at all: the argument parser rejects it with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet` and exits 1.\n\n### Purpose\n\nAdd a `--quiet`/`-q` flag that prints exactly the level line the report already carries, so a caller gets the answer in one line without a parser and without changing any existing invocation.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add `--quiet` and `-q` to the argument parser, held in a `QUIET_OUTPUT` global.\n- Add an `output_quiet()` function that prints the existing level line.\n- Route the output dispatch so quiet sits between JSON and the full report.\n- Document the flag in the file header OPTIONS block and in `--help`.\n- Prove the default report is unchanged against recorded baseline line counts.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Scoring, thresholds, confidence and phase logic - the flag changes presentation only\n- The JSON schema - `--json` keeps precedence and its document is unchanged\n- stderr and exit codes - errors still print and exit 1 under `--quiet`\n- An environment variable or config file - a flag is explicit, local to the invocation and discoverable in `--help`\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Parse the flag, add the quiet output function, route the dispatch, document the option |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--quiet` and `-q` are accepted; with either one, stdout is exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and the exit code is 0 |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout and exit codes are byte-identical to the current script for every documented invocation: 16 lines for the text report, 36 for `--json`, 12 with `--no-recommend-phases` |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-003 | `--help` and the file header OPTIONS block list `--quiet`; `--help` still exits 0 |\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet --json` emits the JSON document unchanged, because JSON takes precedence over quiet |\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` leaves stderr alone: `--loc 0 --files 0` still prints its error to stderr, prints no level line, and exits 1 |\n| REQ-006 | The script keeps `set -euo pipefail` and passes `bash -n` |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db` prints one line and exits 0\n- **SC-002**: `--loc 500 --files 15 | wc -l` prints 16, `--json | wc -l` prints 36 and `--no-recommend-phases | wc -l` prints 12, matching today's measurements\n- **SC-003**: `--quiet --json --loc 100 --files 3` matches `--json --loc 100 --files 3` byte for byte\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2 or newer | The script cannot run on a shell without `[[ ]]` and arrays | Keep the change inside the constructs the script already uses |\n| Risk | A caller keys on the exact level line, so a wording change breaks the parse | Med | Reuse the line verbatim and pin its text with AC-001 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable time: the same one-pass scoring plus one `echo`, with the run staying under 50 ms\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: N/A - the script reads no credentials, files or network; it parses numeric arguments only\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Exit codes are unchanged: 0 on success and 1 on any usage or validation error\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--loc 0 --files 0` keeps its current behavior - error to stderr, exit 1, no level line\n- Maximum length: `--loc` and `--files` are digit-validated; values above the top threshold cap at 35 and 20 points, and quiet prints the resulting Level 3 line\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A - the script makes no external calls\n- Network timeout: N/A - the script has no network path\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A - the script is a one-shot process with no persisted state\n- Session expiry: N/A - the script holds no session\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 3/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~15, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 2/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N (additive flag) |\n| Research | 3/20 | Read one script end to end |\n| Multi-Agent | 1/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 1/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **10/100 measured** | **Level 0 by score; filed at Level 3** |\n\nThe measured score maps to Level 0. `create.sh --level 3` fixed the documentation level for this authoring bench, so the packet carries Level 3 documents for a Level 0 change. The extra documentation is the cost of the bench, not a claim that the change is risky.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | A caller parses the level line and a wording change breaks it | M | L | Reuse the line verbatim; AC-001 pins the exact text |\n| R-002 | `--quiet` accidentally suppresses an error message | M | L | Errors are written before the output dispatch; AC-007 covers the error path |\n| R-003 | The quiet line drifts from the report's first line | L | M | Build both from the same globals and compare them during verification |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One line for a build script (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script that picks the documentation level, **I want** one line on stdout, **so that** I can read the level without a parser.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (AC-001, AC-002).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: A discoverable flag (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator running `--help`, **I want** the quiet option listed with the others, **so that** I can find it without reading the source.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (AC-005).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- None. `--quiet` leaves stderr and exit codes alone (section 8), and `--json` keeps precedence (ADR-002 in `plan.md`).\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md`, ADR-001 and ADR-002\n\n---\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":60,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":60}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
